</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e42ab36-dda4-11f1-9579-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T042126Z-330483324-RC2DLOAYE7CM-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-STRIKE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e42ab36-dda4-11f1-9579-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T042126Z-330483324-RC2DLOAYE7CM-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-STRIKE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b18d818e-dda3-11f1-869c-372a8ce69f43</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:29:33 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e42ab36-dda4-11f1-9579-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T042126Z-330483324-RC2DLOAYE7CM-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-STRIKE.jpg">© Violeta Santos Moura, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Abu Dhabi peace talks show US, Europe and Ukraine are ‘aligned’, NATO chief says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261111-live-ukraine-russia-hold-second-day-of-us-mediated-talks-in-abu-dhabi</link><description>
The US-mediated Ukraine-Russia peace talks in Abu Dhabi showed that the US, Ukraine and Europe were "aligned and united", NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte said Wednesday. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky arrived in Dublin Wednesday evening to meet European leaders and US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner following the negotiations. Read our liveblog to see how the day's events unfolded.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fedbd5f8-de07-11f1-8bb0-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315672409439.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fedbd5f8-de07-11f1-8bb0-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315672409439.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">50be60e2-dd64-11f1-be69-21fca179e78f</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:22:45 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fedbd5f8-de07-11f1-8bb0-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315672409439.jpg">© Ole Berg-Rusten, NTB via AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Europe not going anywhere': G7 summit a 'signal to Russians' that Europe is standing behind Ukraine</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe-not-going-anywhere-g7-summit-a-signal-to-russians-that-europe-is-standing-behind-ukraine</link><description>
As the US Midterms reshape Ukraine diplomacy, Annette Young is pleased to welcome James Townsend, Atlantic Council's Senior Advisor at the Scowcroft Center's for Transatlantic Security Initiative. "Europe is not going anywhere." With this unequivocal assessment, Townsend, taking place alongside trilateral negotiations in Abu Dhabi. While the latter may command international attention, Townsend argues that the Paris meeting carries a message of its own: European support for Ukraine is intended to remain durable, and Moscow should not mistake diplomatic negotiations for an erosion of Western resolve. The G7 meeting therefore emerges as more than a forum for diplomatic declarations. It represents a test of Western cohesion, the credibility of future military and financial assistance, and Washington's willingness to endorse a common position on Ukraine. As Townsend emphasizes, the final communiqué will also be "a test as far as what Marco Rubio is going to agree to put his name to."
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/901d8746-dd4f-11f1-9396-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-130347-130856-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/901d8746-dd4f-11f1-9396-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-130347-130856-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">961faa84-dd4f-11f1-8d80-3ba878fe5186</guid><pubDate>Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:57:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Asymmetric cyberwarfare: 'States increasingly use cyberattacks in support of their other objectives'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asymmetric-cyberwarfare-states-increasingly-use-cyberattacks-in-support-of-their-other-objectives</link><description>
Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Rafe Pilling, Director of Threat Intelligence in the Sophos Counter Threat Unit (CTU). How can a minor cyber intrusion disrupt food supplies, expose the weaknesses of industrial infrastructure and become an instrument of geopolitical confrontation? The cyberattacks targeting supermarket refrigeration systems in France offer a revealing illustration of how digital vulnerabilities can translate into tangible economic disruption and potentially broader threats to society. As Pilling explains, “With a small amount of effort from the adversary side, they can create a large impact, in this case destroying food and producing empty shelves or empty refrigeration units in stores.” This asymmetry lies at the heart of contemporary cyber conflict, in which modest technical interventions can produce disproportionate economic and operational consequences
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/54ae7cc8-dd4b-11f1-a4f5-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-150753-151904-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/54ae7cc8-dd4b-11f1-a4f5-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-150753-151904-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5a443a88-dd4b-11f1-adec-d3e0d8281655</guid><pubDate>Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:25:45 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
